# S2 — ordered-comparison worked example (lara-syntax@0.3)
#
# The second strict-certificate worked example, and the first exercising the
# ord@1 backend: the most common claim shape in ML methodology papers — "this
# number beats that number" — carried end to end from two measured cells to a
# comparative claim.
#
# The layering is the point (plan §3.6). a1 is the STRICT step: the bare
# ordered comparison num_lt(0.71, 0.74), re-checked by ord@1 in exact rational
# arithmetic from a certificate naming its two consulted premise slots. a2 is
# the DEFEASIBLE bridge: it consumes that strict conclusion together with the
# binding leaf that says which systems and measurand those two numbers belong
# to, and concludes better(sys_new, sys_base, accuracy, imagenet_val).
#
# The factivity firewall, concretely: the certificate discharges ONLY that
# each cited premise's numeric literal equals the corresponding goal numeral
# and that 0.71 < 0.74. It asserts nothing about whether either measurement is
# true — that stays with the defeasible observed leaves. You cannot argue with
# the arithmetic; you argue with the measurements and with the binding.
#
# WHAT CHANGED AT @0.3. Both arguments, and the sub-claim they turn on, are now
# written as one `comparison` block. The layering above is unchanged — the block
# still produces a strict a1 and a defeasible a2, and the elaborated Unit is
# byte-identical to the hand-written @0.2 source this replaced. What is gone is
# everything the author was previously required to write but was not claiming:
# the num_lt argument order, the two certificate slot indices, and both θ
# vectors. The author now states the two cells, the binding, and the direction;
# the policy's measurand polarity and comparison scheme (ord-v1.policy.lara)
# determine the rest.
#
# The sub-claim c2 did NOT vanish with its declaration. Its prose, its author
# attestation, and its id all reach the Unit and none can be machine-invented,
# so they are declared in the block's `claims` sub-block; only its `formal` —
# the arithmetic — is generated.
#
# Expected verdict is at the bottom of this file (golden oracle).

artifact ord_demo at sha256:5252525252525252525252525252525252525252525252525252525252525252
policy ord-v1
use backends [ord@1]

# --- The comparative claim the bridge rule delivers -------------------------------------------

claim c1
  nl      = "sys_new outperforms sys_base on ImageNet-val accuracy (0.74 vs 0.71)"
  formal  = better(sys_new, sys_base, accuracy, imagenet_val)
  binding = { author = alice, rationale = "Ordered comparison of two reported accuracy cells.", audit-status = reviewed }

# --- The two measured cells the certificate consults ------------------------------------------
# One leaf per reported cell. These carry the raw numbers; their truth is observed evidence,
# never certified content. They are also the only place a number is written: the comparison
# below names these leaves and reads the values out of them.

leaf e1 : reports(exp1, score_cell(sys_base, accuracy, imagenet_val, 0.71))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [evidence/tables/accuracy.md#row=sys_base]

leaf e2 : reports(exp1, score_cell(sys_new, accuracy, imagenet_val, 0.74))
  kind       = observed
  provenance = ai-executed
  refs       = [evidence/tables/accuracy.md#row=sys_new]

# The binding the bridge rule needs: which systems and measurand those two numbers belong to.
# The bare comparison cannot say this — as a proposition num_lt(0.71, 0.74) is the same atom
# whichever measurands produced the numbers — so the identity enters as attested evidence.
#
# This leaf is NEVER generated. It is an attestation with an author and a provenance tag; a
# `comparison` block that synthesized it would manufacture evidence nobody signed, and the thing
# examples/S4 attacks would be something the compiler invented.

leaf e3 : comparison_setup(sys_new, sys_base, accuracy, imagenet_val, 0.74, 0.71)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [evidence/tables/accuracy.md#caption]

# --- The comparison: one block, two arguments -------------------------------------------------
# `relation` plus the measurand's declared polarity select the scheme; the scheme names the
# strict rule (beats_recheck) and the bridge (beats_baseline). From `result`/`baseline` the
# elaborator reads the two cells out of the named leaves — the same premiseCell helper ord@1
# itself uses — and generates:
#
#   * claim c2, whose formal is num_lt(0.71, 0.74) — the goal the (strictly-better,
#     higher-is-better) cell of the lookup table calls for, in that argument order;
#   * arg a1, the strict step, with the ord@1 certificate and its two 0-based (prem i) slots;
#   * arg a2, the defeasible bridge, consuming the strict conclusion plus the binding leaf.
#
# Both argument ids are author-declared rather than derived, and that is load-bearing: S4
# attacks this bridge by id and position. The sub-claim's prose interpolates the SAME leaves the
# arithmetic reads, so a number quoted in prose is guaranteed to be the number the cited
# evidence carries — it cannot go stale independently.

comparison : better(sys_new, sys_base) on accuracy @ imagenet_val
  relation = strictly-better
  recheck  = a1
  bridge   = a2
  result   = e2
  baseline = e1
  binding  = e3
  claims c2
    nl      = "The reported baseline accuracy {cell e1} is strictly below the reported system accuracy {cell e2}"
    binding = { author = alice, audit-status = reviewed }
  supports c1

status c1

# ==============================================================================================
# EXPECTED VERDICT (golden oracle)
# ==============================================================================================
# Compiled AF: a1 (the strict ord@1 comparison step) and a2 (the defeasible bridge, with a1 as
# a sub-argument), both unattacked -> in (grounded).
# Claim status:
#   c1 -> justified (a2 is a complete argument for it, with no attackers)
